          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q51",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q51",
          "passage": "An analysis by Alain Elayi and colleagues of\ncoins minted in Sidon in the fifth and fourth\ncenturies BCE reveals a change in their\ncomposition over time: while a coin from circa\n450 BCE contains about 98% silver and 1%\ncopper, a coin from 367 BCE (the end of\nBa'alšillem II's reign) contains 74.2% silver and\n24.7% copper, giving it a relatively yellowish\nappearance that traders would have noticed.\nBecause coins with a silver content below 80%\nwere widely considered unsuitable for trade,\nElayi et al. speculate that a crisis in confidence\nin the currency occurred in Sidon around 367\nBCE, which was likely relieved—despite Sidon's\npersistent oppressive financial obligations—as a\nresult of Ba'alšillem II's successor Abd'aštart I's\ndecision to .",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "keep the amount of silver in Sidonian coins\nconsistent with that in coins minted in 367\nBCE but decrease their weight.",
            "proclaim that the percentage of silver in\ncoins suitable for trade would be raised to\na threshold higher than 80%.",
            "fund the mining of some copper deposits\nthat were not available to Ba'alšillem II.",
            "begin minting heavier coins with a\nproportion of silver to copper similar to\nthat in coins minted in 367 BCE."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q51). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
