          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q72",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q72",
          "passage": "The lacy hawkmoth, a large-bodied moth,\ndefends itself against the eastern red bat and\nother insect-eating bats, which use echolocation\nto hunt, by emitting ultrasonic clicks that can,\nfor instance, disrupt the bats' echolocation\nsignals. To investigate moths' defense\nultrasound—which researchers had thought was\nexclusive to tiger moths, hawkmoths and one\nspecies of geometrid moths—Akito Y. Kawahara\net al. recorded the responses of moths from 252\ngenera, representing most families of\nlarge-bodied moths, to audio playback of bat\necholocation. The researchers found that 52 of\nthe genera, including several genera belonging to\nthe geometer family, produced defensive\nultrasonic clicks. This result suggests that",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
