          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q128",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q128",
          "passage": "A soil's mícrobial community (the microbial\ntaxa present in their relative abundances) is\nknown to affect plants' nutrient acquisition.\nCorrine Walsh and colleagues hypothesized that\nmicrobial communities could also affect plants'\nflavor chemistry, including volatiles and\nsecondary metabolites like glucosinolates.\nRecognizing that soll moisture varies by location\nin the wild and could influence plants'\nchemistry, Walsh et al. introduced distinct\nmicrobial communities to individually potted\nmustard plants (Brassica juncea) growing in a\ncontrolled environment, then measured the\nplants' glucosinolates, like 3-methylthiopropyl\nand allyl (the most prominent), that create the\nspicy and bitter flavors in mustard. This method\nthus enabled the researchers to",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "distinguish variations in glucosinolates due\nto differences in microbial communities\nfrom those that in a natural setting may\nbe due to differences in soil moisture.",
            "disentangle the influence of microbial\ncommunities on soil moisture from their\ninfluence on glucosinolate content in the\nplants.",
            "differences in plants' flavor chemistry\nresult from differences in the level of\nglucosinolates regardless of the soil\nmoisture.",
            "re-create the conditions of studies\nconducted in the wild that found links\nbetween soil microbial communities and\nlevels of 3-methylthiopropyl in mustard\nplants."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q128). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q129",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q129",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q129",
          "passage": "In September of 1989, Indonesia liberalized its\nstock market, meaning that it began allowing\nforeign individuals and businesses to invest\nmoney in Indonesian companies. This was part\nof a wave of stock market liberalizations around\nthe world–Jamaica in 1991, Nigeria in 1995, and\nso on. The standard view among economists at\nthe time was that liberalization would make it\neasier for companies to raise money from\ninvestors. Economist Peter Blair Henry\nexamined the economies of 11 countries that\nwere part of the liberalization wave and found\nthat, on average, companies based in those\ncountries received significant increases in\ninvestment in the three years following\nliberalization, suggesting that",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "companies in Jamaica experienced a\ngreater increase in investment following\nliberalization than did companies in\nIndonesia.",
            "companies in the countries Henry studied\ndid not benefit from liberalization until at\nleast three years after liberalization\noccurred.",
            "economists who held the standard view of\nliberalization failed to anticipate some\nserious negative effects of liberalization.",
            "economists' expectations about the effect\nof liberalization on investment were largely\ncorrect for the countries Henry studied."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q129). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q130",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q130",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q130",
          "passage": "Researchers who study olfaction–the sense of\nsmell–define valence as a person's perception of\nhow pleasant an odor is. Conventional wisdom\nholds that valence is culturally mediated. A\nteam of scientists led by Artin Arshamian\nevaluated this view by testing how people from\nten different places-including the Semelai people\nfrom a small community in the Malay Peninsula\nand the Chachi people from a small community\nin Ecuador-ranked ten odors from most pleasant\nto least pleasant. In general, respondents ranked\nscents similarly regardless of where they lived,\noverwhelmingly choosing the odorant eugenol as\nmore pleasant than caprylic acid. These results\nshow that",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "valence may affect cultural traditions more\nstrongly than researchers had previously\npredicted.",
            "the respondents agreed more often on\nunpleasant odors than they did on\npleasant ones.",
            "the conventional belief that odor\npleasantness can be objectively measured\nis questionable.",
            "the standard view of culture's role in\nolfactory valence may be unsound."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q130). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q131",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q131",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q131",
          "passage": "Rudolf Cesaretti et al, conducted an analysis of\nhistorical urban scaling trends circa 1300CE in\nWestern Europe, focusing on agglomeration\npatterns within medieval settlements. They\nexamined settlements in urban systems across\nWestern Europe, with groupings based on\nregionally distinct political structures, economic\nfocuses, and spatial constraints — including the\naristocratically controlled city-state of Parma in\nnorthern Italy, merchant controlled city-states\nlike Goerlitz in Germany, the wool-trading\ncenters of Lincoln and Canterbury in England,\nand prime trade fair towns like Troyes in France.\nA prevalent trend emerged: as a city's total\nsettled area increased, so did population density\nin its core urban areas. This finding implies that",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "even when they were substantial, known\ninter-regional differences in characteristics\nof urban systems had a limited effect on\nagglomeration patterns in medieval\nWestern European settlements.",
            "the observed relationship is heavily\ninfluenced by urban characteristics that\nwere particular to medieval Western\nEurope, making it unlikely that a similarly\nstrong relationship between area and\npopulation density would be found for\nmedieval settlements in other regions.",
            "the effects of variations in political and\neconomic interests on agglomeration\npatterns were less evident in comparisons\nbetween medieval settlements within a\nregion than in comparisons between\ngroups of settlements across regions of\nWestern Europe.",
            "despite regional differences in their\npolitical and economic systems, medieval\nWestern European settlements shared\ncommon spatial constraints that\nencouraged high population density in core\nurban areas."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q131). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q132",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q132",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q132",
          "passage": "The compositional strategy of Untitled, a 1955\nwork by Cherokee artist Edna Massey, is far\nmore closely aligned with Abstract\nExpressionism — a mid-twentieth-century\nschool of painting dominated by European\nAmerican artists — than with traditionally\nabstract forms of indigenous art, such as\nbeadwork. Few viewers would infer from the\nstylistic attributes of Untitled that Massey was\nIndigenous. In this respect, the work typifies\nIndigenous painters' forays into abstraction\nduring the period. In contrast, the\ncontemporary Caddo artist Chad \"Nishi\" Earles\nassembles abstract compositions out of motifs\ncommon in the traditional ceramics and graphic\nart of his tribe. Thus in Earles's work,\nabstraction has the effect of",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "rendering Indigenous identity more legible\nthan it is in Untitled.",
            "asserting the indigenous origins of motifs\nassociated with Abstract\nExpressionismlation density would be\nfound for medieval settlements in other\nregions.",
            "challenging the dominance of European\nAmerican artists within Abstract\nExpressionism.",
            "reconciling Indigenous and European\nAmerican influences that coexist uneasily\nin Untitled."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q132). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q133",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q133",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q133",
          "passage": "Archaeologists assume that when a major\ndemographic shift interrupts the\nintergenerational transmission of expertise, this\nmanifests in the archaeological record in the\nform of simultaneous reductions in the\ncomplexity of multiple specialized crafting\ntraditions. Inventories of excavation sites from\nthe Alazani River valley and nearby areas dating\nfrom 4000 to 500 BCE show a steep drop\noccurring around 1500 BCE in the number of\nobjects featuring gold filigree, an advanced\ntechnique in which fine threads of gold are\narranged in intricate patterns. The inventories\nalso indicate that advanced copper-alloy\nmetallurgy and most other specialized crafting\ntraditions continued to flourish during this\nperiod, a finding suggesting that",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "disruptions around 1500 BCE in the\nutilization of gold filigree likely occurred in\nthe context of demographic continuity\namong peoples of the Alazani River valley.",
            "a sudden simultaneous decline in artifacts\nfrom multiple specialized crafting\ntraditions is less likely than previously\nassumed to indicate that the transmission\nof expertise was disrupted by demographic\nchanges.",
            "peoples in the Alazani River valley\ncontinued to uphold multiple specialized\ncrafting traditions, including goldsmithing\nand copper-alloy metallurgy, even as\ndemographic shifts occurred.",
            "cross-cultural transmission between\ndistinct demographic groups in the\nAlazani River valley likely explains the\nexpansion of copper-alloy metallurgy\nbeginning around 1500 BCE."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 3, Q133). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q134",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q134",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q134",
          "passage": "Right-handedness is overwhelmingly prevalent in\nhumans. Among studies of laterality in\nnonhuman primates, Dulce D. Shafer's 1988\nstudy of captive gorillas reported no tendency\ntoward right-handedness, while S.G. Lutz-Maki\nand P.F. MacNeilage's 1991 study of captive\nchimpanzees did. However, the latter study\nincluded only 14 individuals, and a\nmeta-analysis of primate-laterality studies\ndemonstrated that a minimum sample size of\n176 individuals is required to be confident that a\nfinding of population-level handedness is not\nmere statistical noise. The claim of\nright-handedness in the 1991 study should\ntherefore be treated skeptically given that",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
