        {
          "id": "satoplam-t03-q141",
          "skill": "Inference",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Draw logical deductions, extensions, or implications based on text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 3 · Q141",
          "passageTitle": "Inference — Q141",
          "passage": "In 2008, the US state of Hawaii enacted rate\nstability regulations (RSRs), constraining\ninsurance companies' latitude to raise premiums\n(the periodic fees policyholders pay to maintain\ninsurance policies) after policies are in effect.\nRSRs are effective at protecting existing\npolicyholders from price volatility, but Naoki\nAizawa and Ami Ko note that since dynamic\npricing of premiums is an important\nrisk-mitigation tool for insurers, RSRs may lead\nsome insurers to scale back or entirely cease\nselling new policies in the affected market,\nthereby reducing the competitive pressure that\ntypically restrains premium prices for new\npolicies. Thus, Hawaii's RSRs may",
          "prompt": "Which choice most logically completes the text?",
          "options": [
            "benefit policyholders at the expense of\nnonpolicyholders seeking to acquire\npolicies.",
            "reduce premium price volatility once\npolicies are in effect but increase risks for\npolicyholders.",
            "prevent large increases in premium prices\nfor new policies despite leading to fewer\ninsurers offering such policies in the\naffected market.",
